          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-a8b1d8589ee9a170\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Keith’s “I love you too” is principally a reciprocal avowal presenting his love for Nikki as true. His steady, sincere delivery and the narration’s description of it as an admission strongly support T. Although the exchange deepens their relationship, the utterance does not itself clearly undertake a commitment, and nothing presents it as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The intimate context gives the reply relational significance, but there is insufficient evidence that its core force includes a commitment rather than an avowal alone.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Keith directly replies “I love you too” to Nikki’s immediately preceding declaration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The reply occurs after mutual intimacy and answers Nikki’s sudden declaration. Keith briefly stills before responding, but then speaks steadily and sincerely. The narration frames both declarations as an honest, vulnerable admission and a release, supporting a genuine reciprocal avowal rather than deception, irony, or merely formulaic reciprocation. His later wish to continue the relationship reinforces the sincerity contextually, without turning this utterance itself into an explicit undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Keith stilled, then smiled. ‘I love you too,’ he said, his voice steady and sincere.”\",\"supports\":\"The direct reciprocal wording and explicit characterization as sincere strongly support truth-conditional avowal; the steady delivery provides no positive evidence of reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty that left them both vulnerable but deeply connected.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration explicitly frames the exchange as an honest admission, supporting T. ‘Release’ describes its emotional effect but does not independently establish expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Keith’s reply follows Nikki’s “I love you,” whose words had “spill[ed] out before she could stop them.”\",\"supports\":\"The word “too” functions as reciprocal agreement with Nikki’s avowal. The reflex-like description applies to Nikki’s initiating utterance, not to Keith’s classified reply.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Keith later says, “this doesn’t have to be goodbye, Nikki.”\",\"supports\":\"This supports ongoing relational interest and contextual sincerity, while remaining separate from any commitment force in the classified utterance itself.\",\"confidence\":0.9}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core reciprocal avowal. The surrounding intimacy, vulnerability, and relational consequences can be described contextually without requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
